14
00:01:02.073 --> 00:01:08.448
Asymptotic notation describes how resource use
grows with input size.

15
00:01:08.448 --> 00:01:16.568
Consider this worked case: An algorithm that
processes each element once and performs a

16
00:01:16.568 --> 00:01:22.948
constant number of operations per element
grows linearly, expressed as O(n).

17
00:01:22.948 --> 00:01:29.181
Separate the formal definition from an
implementation detail, then identify what

18
00:01:29.181 --> 00:01:31.448
evidence would distinguish them.

19
00:01:31.448 --> 00:01:37.885
A precise definition makes later examples
easier to evaluate and revise.

20
00:01:37.994 --> 00:01:45.469
With the foundation in place, examine how Loop
Invariants for Correctness operates in a

21
00:01:45.469 --> 00:01:46.537
concrete sequence.

22
00:01:46.537 --> 00:01:53.069
A loop invariant supports a proof by
initialization, maintenance, and termination.

23
00:01:53.069 --> 00:01:58.688
Consider this worked case: In a
sum-accumulation loop, the invariant 'sum

24
00:01:58.688 --> 00:02:06.351
equals the total of all processed elements'
holds before the loop, remains true after each

25
00:02:06.351 --> 00:02:11.459
iteration, and guarantees the final result
matches the total sum.

26
00:02:11.459 --> 00:02:18.865
Follow every arrow in order and name the state
before and after each transition.

27
00:02:18.865 --> 00:02:24.647
The trace converts an abstract statement into
an inspectable sequence.

28
00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:31.262
Next, compare the choices involved in
Divide-and-Conquer Strategy and make the

29
00:02:31.262 --> 00:02:33.032
decision criteria explicit.

30
00:02:33.032 --> 00:02:40.579
Divide-and-conquer algorithms split a problem,
solve subproblems, and combine their results.

31
00:02:40.579 --> 00:02:48.414
Consider this worked case: Merge sort divides
an array into halves, recursively sorts each

32
00:02:48.414 --> 00:02:52.891
half, and merges the sorted halves back
together.

33
00:02:52.891 --> 00:03:00.502
At each level, the subproblems shrink while
the combine step reconstructs one complete

34
00:03:00.502 --> 00:03:01.673
ordered result.

35
00:03:01.673 --> 00:03:08.838
Compare alternatives against the same
constraints so the evaluation does not move

36
00:03:08.838 --> 00:03:10.032
the goalposts.

37
00:03:10.032 --> 00:03:17.001
Using shared criteria turns a preference into
a defensible technical choice.

38
00:03:17.114 --> 00:03:23.665
Apply Recurrence Relations to a worked
situation instead of treating it as an

39
00:03:23.665 --> 00:03:24.673
isolated definition.

40
00:03:24.673 --> 00:03:29.110
Recurrences describe the cost of recursive
algorithms.

41
00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:41.064
Consider this worked case: The merge sort
recurrence T(n) = 2T(n/2) + O(n) captures the

42
00:03:41.064 --> 00:03:47.439
cost of splitting, solving two subproblems,
and merging.

43
00:03:47.439 --> 00:03:55.247
Work from the concrete inputs to the result,
checking units, types, or invariants at each

44
00:03:55.247 --> 00:03:55.767
step.

45
00:03:55.767 --> 00:04:00.954
The worked case shows both how to apply the
method and how to check it.

46
00:04:01.058 --> 00:04:08.629
Examine the tradeoffs in Graph Representation,
including what improves and what becomes more

47
00:04:08.629 --> 00:04:09.211
difficult.

48
00:04:09.211 --> 00:04:16.523
Graphs can be represented with adjacency
structures for traversal and path algorithms.

49
00:04:16.523 --> 00:04:26.755
Consider this worked case: An adjacency matrix
uses a 2D array where entry (i,j) indicates an

50
00:04:26.755 --> 00:04:33.789
edge between vertex i and vertex j, enabling
direct edge lookup.

51
00:04:33.789 --> 00:04:40.695
State the benefit, the cost, and the condition
under which the tradeoff is acceptable.

52
00:04:40.695 --> 00:04:48.242
Making the tradeoff visible prevents a local
improvement from hiding a larger cost.

53
00:04:48.362 --> 00:04:54.776
Stress-test the reasoning around Data
Structure Invariants with an edge case and

54
00:04:54.776 --> 00:04:57.983
identify which assumption controls the
outcome.

55
00:04:57.983 --> 00:05:04.233
Representation choices and invariants
determine which operations are efficient and

56
00:05:04.233 --> 00:05:06.733
which errors are possible.

57
00:05:06.733 --> 00:05:14.476
Consider this worked case: A stack invariant
requires that the top element is always the

58
00:05:14.476 --> 00:05:21.186
most recently added item, ensuring O(1) push
and pop operations but preventing random

59
00:05:21.186 --> 00:05:21.702
access.

60
00:05:21.702 --> 00:05:28.436
Change one assumption at a time and observe
which part of the reasoning no longer holds.

61
00:05:28.436 --> 00:05:35.655
The edge case reveals the boundary of the
claim without discarding the useful core idea.

62
00:05:35.762 --> 00:05:42.721
Use Sorting and Hashing Foundations to
diagnose a failure by separating symptoms,

63
00:05:42.721 --> 00:05:45.040
causes, and corrective action.

64
00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:53.089
Core methods include sorting, hashing, trees,
graphs, shortest paths, and dynamic

65
00:05:53.089 --> 00:05:53.821
programming.

66
00:05:53.821 --> 00:06:01.331
Consider this worked case: Hash tables map
keys to locations using a hash function,

67
00:06:01.331 --> 00:06:08.305
requiring a collision strategy like chaining
or open addressing to maintain data integrity.

68
00:06:08.305 --> 00:06:16.180
Locate the first point where observed behavior
diverges from expected behavior, then test the

69
00:06:16.180 --> 00:06:17.868
smallest plausible cause.

70
00:06:17.868 --> 00:06:26.055
The diagnosis is strongest when another person
can reproduce the observation and the test.

71
00:06:26.162 --> 00:06:32.786
Connect Dynamic Programming Overview to the
earlier ideas and explain how the pieces work

72
00:06:32.786 --> 00:06:34.205
as one system.

73
00:06:34.205 --> 00:06:40.237
Dynamic programming records solutions to
overlapping subproblems.

74
00:06:40.237 --> 00:06:46.134
Consider this worked case: The Fibonacci
sequence calculation stores previously

75
00:06:46.134 --> 00:06:54.389
computed values in a table to avoid redundant
recursive calls, reducing time complexity from

76
00:06:54.389 --> 00:06:56.158
exponential to linear.

77
00:06:56.158 --> 00:07:05.653
Explain each connection explicitly and verify
that information retains the same meaning

78
00:07:05.653 --> 00:07:07.236
across representations.

79
00:07:07.236 --> 00:07:14.690
The synthesis matters only if every connection
remains supported by the original evidence.

80
00:07:14.810 --> 00:07:21.719
Finish with Practical Analysis Checklist as a
practical checklist that can guide a new

81
00:07:21.719 --> 00:07:22.213
